<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Butrus al-Bustani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Boutros al-Boustani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Butrus al-Bustani was a writer and scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, a major figure of the Nahda, the Arab renaissance. Born a Maronite, he became a Protestant while working for American missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and helped translate the Bible into Arabic. After the 1860 civil war his Nafir Suriyya, the Clarion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, appealed for unity in a Syrian homeland, and in 1863 he founded the secular National School in Beirut, open to all religions. He compiled the dictionary Muhit al-Muhit, founded the journal al-Jinan in 1870 and in 1876 began the encyclopedia Da'irat al-Ma'arif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/butrus-al-bustani.jpg" alt="Butrus al-Bustani" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Mu'allim_Butrus_al-Bustani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Butrus al-Bustani was born in January 1819 into a Maronite Christian family in the village of Dibbiye in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>. His teacher, Father Mikhail al-Bustani, noticed his intelligence and recommended him to Abdullah al-Bustani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and Beiteddine, who sent him at eleven to the school at 'Ayn Warqa in Ghosta. He spent ten years there, learning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and several foreign languages including French, Italian and English.</p><p>In 1840 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and worked first as a dragoman for the British forces helping to evict Ibrahim Pasha from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Later that year American Protestant missionaries hired him as a teacher, and he worked with the mission for most of his life and converted to their Protestant faith. He married Rahil Ata, called the mother of the Nahda, and they had nine children. In the late 1840s he became dragoman of the American Consulate in Beirut, a post he passed to his son Salim in 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With Nasif al-Yaziji he worked under the missionary Eli Smith on the Arabic translation of the Bible later completed by Cornelius Van Allen Van Dyck, learning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and Greek in the process. In 1847 he helped found the Syrian Association, and in a lecture of February 1859 on the literature of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> he called for a revival of Arabic letters. That year he left mission work. After the Maronite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> civil war of 1860 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> he published the irregular paper Nafir Suriyya, voicing the ideal of a Syrian fatherland, and for this he is considered the first Syrian nationalist.</p><p>In 1863 he founded the National School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, which taught Arabic, French, English, Turkish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek and modern sciences, took students of all religions and hired staff for competence rather than faith. It was closed in 1878 as religious solidarity rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He helped found the Syrian Scientific Society in 1868, founded Al-Jinan in 1870, the first important Arabic literary and scientific periodical, and compiled an Arabic dictionary and, with his sons, eleven volumes of an encyclopedia. His works include Muhit al-Muhit and Da'irat al-Ma'arif, begun in 1876.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Bustani died on 1 May 1883. He is known as the Master and Father of the Arabic Renaissance. He argued for one educational system for all the children of the nation, drew on the scientific heritage of the Abbasid age, and favoured Ottomanism within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms as a way for citizens of any religion to take part in the state. His output is credited with helping create modern Arabic expository prose. His writings are kept in the Yafeth Library at the American University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Butrus al-Bustani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Love of the homeland, the watan, became a theme: Rifa’a al-Tahtawi wrote of patriotic duty in Egypt, and <b>Butrus al-Bustani</b> appealed to the people of Syria, Christian, Muslim and Druze, to see themselves as children of one country.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dibbiyeh, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in Beirut, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.67,35.49&amp;year=1860" aria-label="Dibbiyeh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5456 943 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Butrus al-Bustani"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5906 1213h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5906 1213h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.67,35.49&amp;year=1860">What was here in 1860? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
